const FAD_READING=[
{title:"Fad diets",image:"food",alt:"A balanced selection of fresh foods",caption:"The article compares diet promises with evidence.",body:[`Since the 1930s, there have been hundreds of diet plans which make almost unbelievable promises. Diet X will make you slim and Diet Y will make you strong. But is there any truth in these claims? I analysed three examples of fad diets to find the answer to this question.`]},
{title:"1 · Cabbage Soup Diet",image:"veg",alt:"Fresh vegetables",caption:"Cabbage and other vegetables are named in the text.",body:[`This diet appeared in the 1980s, though nobody knows who invented it. It's a simple idea. For one week, eat as much cabbage soup as you like and you will quickly lose weight. This vegetable is cheap and is a good source of fibre, which helps with your body's digestion. The soup can also include other ingredients such as green peppers, onions, celery and carrots.`,`The diet is far from balanced, with not many carbohydrates, no protein and no healthy fat. Also, it's very boring to eat the same thing all day, every day, and the soup recipe often includes a lot of salt, which is bad for you. People on this diet will get slimmer, but usually put on weight fast when they start eating normal food again.`],sub:"Does it work?"},
{title:"2 · Master Cleanse",image:"lemon",alt:"A lemon drink with fresh citrus",caption:"The article describes a drink made with lemon juice and other ingredients.",body:[`Also known as the Lemonade Diet and created in 1941 by an American, Stanley Burroughs, the Master Cleanse usually lasts for two weeks and is popular with Hollywood celebrities. It involves drinking a mixture which contains water, lemon juice, cayenne pepper and maple syrup. It is called a 'detox' diet as they say it improves the health of your body by removing toxins or poisons. However, many leading dieticians tell us there is no scientific evidence that toxins build up inside you.`,`People lose weight because it has very few calories — just 600 a day. However, health experts agree that human bodies need a variety of food to provide different nutrients, which may be why this diet can also cause tiredness and headaches. As with other extreme diets, it is common to put weight on again soon after the end of the diet.`],sub:"Does it work?"},
{title:"3 · Blood Group Diet",image:"salad",alt:"A varied salad of fresh foods",caption:"The section examines claims about food and blood type.",body:[`Here's an idea from 1996 — that the best diet for you depends upon your blood type: A, B, AB or O. In his bestselling book Eat Right 4 Your Type, Peter D'Adamo says that people in each blood group respond differently to food and he provides lists of food that each group should and shouldn't eat. For example, group O can have fish, vegetables, olive oil and fruit but no dairy or beans.`,`Hmm, in fact all four types of diet are quite good for you as they contain fresh, natural food rather than processed meals. However, researchers have found no scientific evidence that a particular blood type needs specific kinds of food.`],sub:"Does it work?"},
{title:"Research conclusion",image:"food",alt:"A variety of fresh foods",caption:"The article ends by asking readers to check the science behind claims.",body:[`So, what has our research shown us? That following a fad diet for a short time doesn't harm you. On the other hand, it probably won't do you much good either. It always makes sense to investigate the science behind the claims.`]}
];
