if(part==="reading")html=html.replace(/<div class="skills-reading-brief">[\s\S]*?<\/div>/,'<div class="skills-reading-brief"><h3>Reading passage · adapted for this practice</h3><p>Okinawa is an island group south of the rest of Japan. Older residents have their own language, although today only older people speak it. The reading reports that 34 people in every 100,000 on Okinawa reach the age of 100; in the USA the figure is 10. Scientists connect the long lives of older Okinawans with a traditional diet, but the text presents this as an explanation rather than proof of a single cause.</p><p>About forty years ago, people ate food produced on the island. Their meals included many green and yellow vegetables and little meat. Pork was eaten on special occasions; people also ate few eggs and drank little milk. Although Okinawa is by the sea, people there ate less fish than many other Japanese people. They also ate less rice and preferred sweet potatoes, which the text says have fewer calories than rice.</p><p>The text says elderly Okinawans rarely get some diseases that are common elsewhere and reports fewer heart problems than in the USA. It then describes a change in daily life: the island is busier and more modern, and teenagers buy fast food and fizzy drinks more often. They eat rice and bread instead of sweet potatoes and vegetables. The article still describes Okinawan teenagers as healthier than teenagers in Japan and elsewhere, but says their diet is less distinct than before. The writer suggests that fewer people may reach 100 in the future.</p></div>');
